; A single declaration-macro instantiation that produces more than the
; budget allows, and the refusal that used to be an out-of-memory kill.
;
; The three budgets `expand.ax` had were all reachable only from
; `expandExpr`, and `expandProgram` runs the DECLARATION phase FIRST -
; so for a template that generates declarations, phase E never starts
; and none of them is ever touched. One axis was covered there anyway:
; `expDeclMacroRounds` bounds DEPTH, and `406-decl-macro-round-limit`
; is the case for it. The axis with nothing on it was SIZE.
;
; Measured before this budget existed, on a three-way fanning-out
; template with an RSS poller as the only brake: 3.4 GB in ONE second,
; then killed by the operating system, with no diagnostic and no
; output at all. That is the shape a program compiling code it did not
; write cannot defend against, because `check` had no lever to bound
; it.
;
; WHY NESTED ITERATION RATHER THAN THAT FAN-OUT. The fan-out needs
; about 5,000 instantiations to cross the budget, and phase D appends
; an expansion frame per instantiation to a table that is read when a
; diagnostic renders and is never popped - so the refusal came out as
; one AXDL line of 115 KB carrying 5,001 copies of one macro's name,
; and this file's golden was 225 KB of it. `expMaxFrames` bounds what
; one diagnostic carries now, so that shape is 3.5 KB rather than 115,
; but 128 identical frames is still 128 repetitions of one fact.
; 80 x 80 pairs from ONE invocation crosses the same budget with ONE
; frame, which is what a reader of this golden actually needs.
;
; The accumulation itself is still there and is still not a defect to
; fix here: the frames are attached post-hoc by span and module, so
; unwinding them empties the table before anything renders and takes
; ten backtrace goldens with it - measured, on the attempt.
;
; It is AX3024 and not a new code because the axis is the one AX3024
; already names - what expansion PRODUCED against what the source
; measured - and its help is already the right advice.
(data Wide
  (K0)
  (K1)
  (K2)
  (K3)
  (K4)
  (K5)
  (K6)
  (K7)
  (K8)
